::STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.1
Question:
A survey of 80 students finds that 62% prefer online notes. What is 62%?
{
~A. A population parameter#A parameter describes the entire population, not just the surveyed sample.
~B. The full population#The full population is the whole group of interest, not a calculated percent.
=C. A sample statistic#The percent was calculated from the sample of 80 students.
~D. A sampling method#A sampling method describes how data are collected.
}

::STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.1
Question:
A district reports that the average class size for all district classes is 24 students. What is 24?
{
~A. A sample statistic#A statistic would come from only a sample of classes.
~B. A convenience sample#A convenience sample is a selection method, not a population value.
=C. A population parameter#The number describes all district classes, so it is a parameter.
~D. A random sample#A random sample is a way to choose data, not the value itself.
}

::STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.2
Question:
Why does random sampling help produce representative data?
{
~A. It only chooses people who volunteer#Volunteer samples can be biased because only willing people respond.
~B. It guarantees every sample has exactly the same results#Random samples still vary; they do not produce identical results.
~C. It removes the need to define the population#The population must still be defined before sampling.
=D. It gives members of the population a fair chance to be selected#Random selection reduces selection bias and helps the sample reflect the population.
}

::STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L01_QZ_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.2
Question:
Which sample is most likely to be biased?
{
=A. Asking only friends in your lunch group#Friends in one lunch group are easy to reach but may not represent the population.
~B. Drawing student IDs from a complete list#A complete list and random draw can support fair sampling.
~C. Using a random number generator with a full roster#A random generator with a full roster supports fair selection.
~D. Randomly selecting names from all grade levels#Random selection across grade levels supports representativeness.
}

